// Placeholders: the whole "mcpServers" object. Keep ONLY the servers the team accepted in
// Phase 3, and drop this file to `{"mcpServers": {}}` when they accepted none. An empty skeleton
// is still worth writing: it is the committed, discoverable place the next server goes.
//
// Becomes .mcp.json at the repository ROOT. It is committed — that is the point. Project scope is
// how a server reaches the whole team instead of living in one person's ~/.claude.json.
//
// NEVER WRITE A SECRET HERE. Every credential is an ${ENV_VAR} reference, and the variable name
// goes in README.md so a teammate knows what to export. A token committed to .mcp.json is a token
// in the history.
//
// EXPANSION, verified against the current docs:
//   ${VAR}            expands to the environment variable
//   ${VAR:-default}   expands to VAR, or to the default when it is unset
// Expansion works in command, args, env, url and headers — and nowhere else.
//
// NEVER GIVE A CREDENTIAL A DEFAULT. `${SOME_TOKEN:-}` turns "the variable is not set" into "an
// empty token was supplied", and the failure moves from a clear startup error to an authentication
// error the user has to chase. A bare ${VAR} is correct for everything the user must export. The
// `:-` form is for one thing only: a value that has a genuinely sensible fallback — see the note
// on ${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR} below, and the one deliberate exception marked in ark_context7.
//
// ABSOLUTE PATHS DO NOT COME FROM ${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}. Claude Code sets that variable in the
// SERVER's environment, not in its own, so in a project-scoped .mcp.json it has nothing to expand
// from at read time: ${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR:-.} always resolves to the default, `.`. The default is
// mandatory — without it the entry is left unexpanded — but the result is a RELATIVE path.
// Verified: a SQLite DSN needs an absolute path, so `sqlite://${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR:-.}/app.db`
// becomes `sqlite://./app.db` and the server fails with "unable to open database file".
// A machine-specific absolute path is machine-specific data. Treat it like a credential: put it
// in an environment variable with no default, and put the export line in README.md.
//
// WRITING THIS FILE DOES NOT CONNECT THE SERVERS. They sit at "Pending approval" until the user
// trusts the workspace. Worse, `enableAllProjectMcpServers` and `enabledMcpjsonServers` committed
// in .claude/settings.json are IGNORED in an untrusted folder, so a cloned repo cannot approve
// its own servers. Report control status as "written, pending approval" and give the user the
// step: run `claude` in the repo, accept the trust dialog, then `/mcp` to check.
//
// DO NOT PIN VERSIONS in the npx args. `-y <package>` resolves the current release; a hardcoded
// version is a bug the day after it is written.
//
// AND DO NOT PASS A FLAG YOU HAVE NOT CONFIRMED. `-y <package>` resolving the current release
// cuts both ways: a flag that existed when this template was written may be gone. DBHub's
// `--readonly` is the worked example — it now exits with "no longer supported" and the server
// never starts. Confirm each flag against the resolved package before writing it. See
// references/mcp-servers.md, "Confirming a flag", for how to do that without hanging.
//
// JSON DOES NOT ALLOW COMMENTS. Strip every // line before writing.
// ---8<--- everything above this line is instructions for you; delete it ---8<---
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "ark_azdevops": {
      "type": "stdio",
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@azure-devops/mcp", "${ADO_ORGANIZATION}", "--authentication", "pat"],
      "env": { "PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN": "${ADO_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN}" }
    },
    "ark_mslearn": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/api/mcp"
    },
    "ark_context7": {
      "type": "stdio",
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@upstash/context7-mcp"],
      "env": { "CONTEXT7_API_KEY": "${CONTEXT7_API_KEY:-}" }
    },
    "_comment_context7": "The empty default above is the ONE exception to the no-default rule: Context7 has an anonymous tier, so an unset key degrades to lower rate limits instead of failing. Delete this comment key along with the rest of the header.",
    "ark_dbhub": {
      "type": "stdio",
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@bytebase/dbhub", "--transport", "stdio", "--dsn", "${APP_DSN}"]
    }
  }
}
